{"id": "sdqa0100", "pool": "sdqa", "arm": "local", "tier": "never", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 658079636, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "dc9d95c8617de77ddb5971c8746f5327f6cd47b10e39975e824f2c8f1acf9b9a", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 7, "query": "How do thermostats work?", "input_path": "/data/sdqa_audio/sdqa0100.wav", "input_sha256": "a4f34bb1b84a9a1627361fd8d508b452490a1ed118152546bff6e44738aef8df", "input_samples": 70381, "input_s": 4.399, "n_real_chunks": 5, "score": 0.2161, "act": 0.9989, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "A thermostat is a device that helps control the temperature in your home or office. It works by sensing the current temperature and turning your heating or cooling system on or off to keep it at the set level. Most modern thermost ats have a sensor, like a bimetallic strip or an electronic component, that changes its position as the temperature changes. When the room gets too hot or too cold, the thermostat triggers the HVAC system to run until the temperature returns to the desired setting. Some advanced models can learn your schedule and adjust automatically, while others connect to the internet for remote control. In short, it’s like a smart switch that keeps your space comfortable without constant manual adjustment.", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 4, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 43, "feed_lag_max_ms": 0, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 0, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/sdqa/audio/sdqa0100_local_local.wav", "sha256": "37e4e590be4ad61b29fde761772865df5bf39bb6d9c8328980c809c60d4eec10", "sr": 24000, "seconds